.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-LIST-PERMISSIONS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-list-permissions \- list every permission a role can hold
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam list-permissions
.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints the whole permission vocabulary: every right that
.BR euclid-cli-eam-create-role (1)
can put into a role, as
.IR <module>:<action> .
.PP
A permission is the pair of headers the request it authorizes already
carries \-
.I x\-euclid\-target
and
.I x\-euclid\-action
\- joined by a colon. So
.I ens:publish\-message
is not a name invented for the access model; it is the request itself,
spelled the way the wire spells it.
.SH WHY IT IS GENERATED, NOT WRITTEN DOWN
The list comes from what the modules actually dispatch, checked against
their dispatch tables on every build. A vocabulary maintained separately
from the actions it describes drifts the first time a module gains one,
and the drift is invisible in both directions: a new action simply cannot
be granted, or a permission names something that no longer exists and
quietly grants nothing.
.PP
So this is not a copy of what can be granted. It is what can be granted.
.SH THE MODULES THAT ARE MISSING
Two modules have no permissions at all, and are named in the response's
.I unbindableModules
field rather than being silently absent:
.TP
.B emm
The management module \- export, import, backup and restore. It stays
reachable by installation administrators only. See
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1).
.TP
.B emd
The document-store daemon. It dispatches
.IR find\-one / replace\-one / delete
against any collection by name, so a grant of it would reach past every
check every other module makes. It is euclid's own plumbing and nothing
else uses it.
.PP
Nothing can grant these, including
.IR *:* .
.BR euclid-cli-eam-check-permission (1)
refuses an action of theirs with "is not a permission any role can hold"
rather than "no grant applies", which is the difference between a
misspelling and a missing grant.
.SH WHO MAY RUN IT
Anybody logged in. Unlike the rest of the role commands, this needs no
administrator privileges: it is the vocabulary, not anybody's access, and
a user asking what could be granted is asking nothing private.
.SH OPTIONS
None.
.SH OUTPUT
.TP
.B permissions
Every permission, sorted.
.TP
.B modules
The modules whose actions can be granted, sorted.
.TP
.B unbindableModules
The two above.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The vocabulary was printed.
.TP
.B 1
The request could not be sent, or there is no valid session. If the
failure was an expired or invalid session (\fIHTTP 401\fR), the stored
credentials file is removed; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again before retrying.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Everything that can be granted
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam list-permissions

# Just what ENS answers, to build a topic-shaped role
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam list-permissions \e
    | grep '"ens:'
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-create-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-get-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-check-permission (1)
